  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 116 PM CST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nAbundantly quiet conditions currently prevail over the area as mid-\nlevel ridging builds in behind the northern stream shortwave. Closer \nto the surface, the area finds itself between areas of low pressure \n(north and west) and high pressure (east), resulting in relatively \nlight southerly flow and WAA. This can also be seen nicely on \nvarious satellite imagery, such as Geocolor or any of the visible \nbands, with budding CU over southern into central Missouri. As has \nbeen well advertised in previous editions of this publication, \ntemperatures today remain expected to be a couple to few degrees \nwarmer than yesterday, topping out in the upper 60s to low 70s for \nmost. \n\nThe quiet conditions do not last terribly long though as the sizable \nwestern CONUS trough begins to dig through the Intermountain West \ntonight. This will produce Lee Cyclogenesis over the Colorado Front \nRange and northward into Wyoming. By Friday afternoon this area of \nsurface low pressure will begin to shoot off to the NE and continue \nto tighten the local pressure gradient. Guidance is in quite good \nagreement (limited spreads) on track and resulting wind speeds. \nGiven the track, western areas of the CWA will be notably windier \nthan eastern areas with sustained winds from 20 to 25+ mph over the \nwest to 10-15 mph over the east and similarly gusts from 30 to 35+ \nover the west to around 20 mph east. Of maybe casual note is that \nwhile it has been very dry lately, RH values appear poised to be \naround the 50s percent, limiting peak fire weather potential. \nRegardless, not the best day for any burns. Temperatures once again \nwell above normal with highs upper 60s to low 70s for most. A few \nsouthern CWA areas may even hit the mid-70s. \n\nInto the weekend, the first real weather maker in a number of days \nhits the area. Aforementioned surface low should be southern Ontario \nby Saturday morning, with its trailing cold front draped back to the \nSW through parts of Minnesota/Iowa/Nebraska/Kansas. A building \nsurface high over the western Northern Plains will aid in beginning \nto push this cold front into the area sometime later Saturday \nevening/night. Existing SPC Day 3 Outlook just brushes a Marginal \nRisk to Atchison and Leavenworth. Available soundings suggest \noverall poor lapse rates, yielding narrow and limited CAPE profiles. \nSo while strong shear will be present, there currently does not \nappear to be enough instability to warrant much concern, plus \nprimarily the overnight passage for most of the area. As we get into \nthe CAM windows here shortly, will continue to monitor for any \npotential increasing CAPE trends. The front will otherwise gradually \nmake its way across the area and should provide at least some \nmeasurable precipitation to most or all of the area. This yields \nPoPs of 80 to 95 percent at times from Saturday evening and through \nthe overnight.\n\nBy Sunday, surface high pressure and more zonal mid-level flow \nsettle in for a few days. With the turn to northerly winds, \ntemperatures plummet back toward and below seasonal norms, down into \nthe 40s for highs and 20s for lows. This also includes dry \nconditions expected through at least mid-week next week.",
